{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbKdfAX62t13MdVnunQ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate `personal_sensitivity_opening_run_lapse` and it passes the self-check (`OK`). Two caveats, though: the PSIS-LOO estimate came out unreliable, and the check doesn't cover convergence or novelty.\n\n**What I changed:** I refined `opening_run_streak_aversion_lapse`, which is statistically tied with the incumbent (1.9× dse behind). The original model has one alternation sensitivity shared by everyone. In mine, each person has their own, drawn from a population distribution. So some engaged people choose almost entirely by how often a sequence switches, and others barely tell the sequences apart that way. Everything else stays the same: personal ideal switching rate, personal weights on balance and longest streak, shared weights on the opening and closing runs, and a personal guessing (lapse) rate.\n\n**Why this change:** the critique's only discrepancy is that people differ in how consistently they agree with the majority choice more than the incumbent produces (observed SD 0.177 vs 0.162 predicted). It's the strongest one there, but it is weak: raw p = 0.028, and it does not survive the false-discovery correction (q = 0.213).\n\n**Self-check result:** on a short single-chain test fit (100 draws), ELPD-LOO was −2315.7, versus the incumbent's −2333.8. That's only a rough sign, since the test fit is not a production fit.\n\n**Open risks:**\n- **Unreliable LOO estimate:** 7% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. This may just be the short fit. If it persists in admission's full fit, the model can still be admitted, but it can't be exported as the winner.\n- **Convergence:** not checked. This change adds a 400-slot set of per-person parameters, and the sensitivity may trade off against each person's ideal rate and lapse rate.\n- **Novelty:** not checked.\n\nFiles are in `e